demonstrable	one
nonexist_contradiction	one
conceivable_nonexistent	one
conceived_existent	one
# Unsure about conceivable_nonexistent. S2 speaks of what is "distinctly conceivable", meaning a clear and coherent conception. S3 says only that we "can also conceive" a being as non-existent, which could mean merely imagining it away.
# If S3's "conceive" is the looser sense, P3 would pass from "conceive" as imagine to "conceive" as distinctly conceive. But the text gives no sign that S3 means less than S2. The usual reading of the passage takes both as the same notion of conceivability, so I left it as one sense with no bridge.
